  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Europium&nbsp;has an atomic number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>63</mn></math> and a stable electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>Ytterbium, on the other hand, has an atomic number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn></math> and a stable electronic configuration of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>14</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>Both have high <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>rd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;ionisation energy due to half filled &amp; fully filled configuration respectively.</p>"
